  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\n(Tuesday Night - Friday Night)\n\nDeterministic guidance shows the mid/upper level ridge weakening and \nshifting slightly to the southwest Tuesday night into Wednesday. \nThere is also a pretty coherent signal of another \"ridge-runner\" \nmidlevel shortwave trough to move across the Mississippi River early \nWednesday morning. Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected \ndownstream of this feature, which should be at least slightly \nfarther southwest than the MCS expected Tuesday morning. The core of \nthis MCS should miss us to the northwest, but parts along/east of \nthe Mississippi River may see these showers and storms on the \nsouthwestern flank. In addition, weak moisture convergence on the \nnose of a veering low-level jet should get at least some scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms farther to the west. The combination of \nincreased mid/high level cloud cover and aforementioned rain chances \nreally puts into question high temperatures and peak heat index \nvalues across much of the region. Areas in southeast \nMissouri/southwest Illinois are least likely to be impacted by \nmorning clouds/rain chances and therefore have the best chances of \nseeing another day with 105+ heat index values. There were no \nchanges made to the heat headlines, but at least parts of the area \nmay need to be cancelled early if current indications of \nclouds/storms hold true.\n\nThere remains uncertainty with how the synoptic front evolves mid-\nlate week. A preponderance of guidance shifts the boundary southward \ninto the area late Wednesday/Wednesday night. The front should then \nstall out somewhere across the region through Friday, but it may be \nmodulated by subsequent rounds of convection. Speaking of which, \nthere is a strong signal for widespread convection along/north \nof the quasi-stationary front Thursday night into Friday \nmorning. Parts of northeast Missouri and west-central Illinois \nhave the best chance (50-70%) of showers and thunderstorms \nduring this time period. This convection could in turn help push\nthe front back southward a bit and lead to cooler temperatures \non Friday, but the exact location of the front is not certain at\nthis time range. Needless to say, the precise high temperatures\n(and heat index values) really each day Wednesday through \nFriday are pretty uncertain. The inter-quartile range of the NBM\nshows this uncertainty quite well, with about a 5-10F range \nbetween the 25th/75th percentiles and 10- 20F between the \n10th/90th percentiles.\n\n\n(Saturday - Sunday)\n\nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement that the mid/upper level \nridge will amplify back over the Mississippi Valley early this \nweekend, with leeside cyclogenesis across the Plains. This should \nhelp push the quasi-stationary boundary back north of the area as a \nwarm front. Widespread heat and humidity should return to the bi-\nstate area as a result, with highs most likely rebounding into the \nmid to upper 90s for many locations. Peak heat index values of 105+ \nare expected each day roughly along/south of I-70. \n\n\n(Sunday Night - Next Monday)\n\nThe synoptic pattern may cycle once again heading into early next \nweek as ensemble guidance portrays a flattening mid/upper level \nridge. This likely will allow a cold front to push into the area \nsometime Sunday night into next Monday, bringing with it a returning \nchance (20-40%) of showers and thunderstorms along with temperatures \n(and heat index values) cooling back closer to normal.",
